t the code status across releases, rel04.1 is the earliest incomplete release with actual source code that can be generated. The extended ls features like -l, -R, -t, -r, and -S aren't implemented yet, so that's where I should focus the task. I need to be careful about which requirements I reference without the full PRD content, but proposing a task for ls-extended seems like the most practical path forward given my constraints. I see the constraint now—design decision 7 explicitly forbids the generation pipeline from writing to docs/, so I can't propose PRD tasks for the pending releases. Looking at what's actually available, the early releases all have existing code in source_code, but rel04.1 has spec_complete status with only partial implementation—the ls command has core features but the extended features haven't been coded yet. Since prd008-ls.yaml is already spec_complete, I can propose a task to implement those missing ls extended features.\n\nThe stitch agent will read the PRD as required context and implement based on what it finds there. I need to reference the specific requirement IDs in my task description, but I can't know those without reading the PRD first—though the architecture notes suggest the specs are organized by core (rel02.0) and extended (rel02.1) sections, and there are corresponding use cases like rel04.1-uc001-ls-extended that should guide the implementation.\n\nI can point the stitch agent to the test suite at docs/specs/test-suites/test-rel-04.1.yaml and the PRD at docs/specs/product-requirements/prd008-ls.yaml in the required_reading, so it understands what needs to be built. The challenge is that I need to anchor the task to specific requirement IDs like \"prd001 R1.1\" but don't have visibility into what those IDs actually are for the extended features.\n\nRather than guessing at requirement IDs, I should focus on what I can verify from the existing code—the test file only covers R1.2, R1.3, R1.4, and R6.2, but the implementation ac