hin the 5-7 file limit, though I should verify the acceptance criteria align with the scope. I'm settling on 4 requirements, 5 acceptance criteria, and 3 design decisions—the constraint caps PRD sub-requirements at 4, so this works within those bounds. Now I need to write out the project state summary, reasoning about priorities, and the full task proposal.\n\nChecking whether there's a test suite file for the cat test—the code_status shows an empty testdir, so the test suite might be embedded in the PRD itself rather than as a separate file. I'll reference the PRD and existing code to avoid confusion, then finalize my answer by reconsidering which use case to prioritize based on the earliest incomplete releases.\n\nLooking at the release timeline, 01.1 (cat) stands out as the earliest with a clear testing gap: R2, R3, and R4 are implemented but untested, while 01.2 and 01.3 already have complete test coverage. So proposing tests for the missing cat requirements makes the most sense.\n\nI should also check du and sponge's coverage—du is missing tests for symlink handling and SIGPIPE, while sponge has gaps in buffer size and other requirements—but since cat comes first chronologically, that's where to focus.\n\nFor the test file, I'm modifying the existing cat_test.go rather than creating a new one, which aligns with the project pattern. Looking at the line counts, each test function runs about 20-30 lines, so adding four new tests should keep things reasonable.\n\nI'm noticing the existing tests use multiple cases within each function—like TestCatFileRead with 2 cases and TestCatStdinRead with 3 cases. I should follow that same approach: TestCatLineNumbering with 2-3 cases covering single files, multi-line content, and blank lines; TestCatNumberNonblank with 2-3 cases for the -b flag alone and combined with -n; and similar coverage for the squeeze and show-ends tests.\n\nThat gives me roughly 8-12 DiffTest cases total across 4 functions, which should land around 130